    "title": "Concept 1: Perfect Square Factors",
    "explanation": "<p class=\"text-sm leading-6 text-muted-foreground\">A perfect square factor is a number that is a square of a whole number, like 4, 9, 16, or 25. These numbers can be taken out of a square root to simplify surds. For example, when simplifying <em>√12</em>, we look for perfect square factors of 12. Since 4 is a perfect square and 12 = 4 × 3, we can write <em>√12 = √(4×3) = √4 × √3 = 2√3</em>.</p>",
